// Branch coverage for crypto: its own tests, plus the shapes no test drives.
//
//   deno task coverage:crypto
//
// This was crypto's `cov.ts` until 2026-08-20 — `issues/system/0161`, `issues/system/0222`. The ledger
// is `cov_ledger.wac` beside this file and the reporting is `tools/wac/covledger.wac`, shared.
//
// ## The tests are the workload now, and that is the whole change
//
// The TypeScript could not call them. `harness/wacCoverage.ts`'s `runTestExports` skips any test taking
// an argument — `fn.length > 0` — because `instrument` and `wacBind` cannot supply a capability: a `Cli`
// is built by the wac side against a shared-memory bridge that only a real host sets up. So **64 of
// this package's 152 returning tests were unreachable from its own coverage driver**, and it made up
// the difference with about six hundred lines of hand-built probe calls.
//
// `wac covdump` runs the ordinary program path (`issues/system/0221`), so `main` here has a real `Core`
// and `Cli` and simply calls them. Measured: the tests alone reach **1100 of 1173** points, against the
// TypeScript's 974 of 1053 — more points seen and fewer missed, because a test file pulls in modules
// the seven probes never imported.
//
// **So `MEASURED_BY_THE_BINARY` is gone.** That list existed for exactly one entry: all five of
// `mlkem_test.wac`'s tests take `(Core core, Cli cli)`, so the driver called none of them and reported
// fifty of `mlkem.wac`'s points uncovered, while `wac test --coverage` read 125 of 132. The fix was to
// spawn the binary, take that measurement and assert the figure. Here `mlkem.wac` reads **131 of 132**
// in the same counter array as everything else — better than the number that was being asserted, since
// the shared paths are covered by the rest of the run too. A measurement in one place beats a
// measurement asserted from another.
//
// ## What the probe calls are still for
//
// Not everything. The tests leave 73 points, and they are concentrated: `sha1.wac` 15,
// `weierstrass.wac` 14, `rsa.wac` 11, `rsagen.wac` 8, `aesctr.wac` 6. Those are the field boundaries
// and streaming shapes a test asserts *an answer* about at one or two lengths, where coverage wants
// every arm. So the probe half stays, aimed at those, and the rest of the six hundred lines went with
// the tests that already cover them.
//
// ## A trap is an export, and a test that wants one is a sweep
//
// 130 of this package's 283 tests are `test_traps_*`: a guard against a caller error is exactly the code
// nothing exercises by accident, and one export per case is what lets a test say it *wants* a trap. A
// trap ends the call, so they cannot be called from `main` — `trapCase` is one sweep over all 130
// (`spec/cli/wac.md`, `[§wac-cli-covdump-sweep-4tn8mr6]`).
//
// **The exercise does not assert that a forgery is refused.** `cov.ts` had a `mustTrap` that failed if a
// call returned, on the reasoning that catching silently would turn "the forgery was accepted" into a
// passing coverage run. That reasoning is right and the claim belongs where it can fail:
// `aead_test.wac`'s `test_traps_on_a_flipped_tag_bit` and its fifteen siblings, run by `wac test`, where
// a case that does not trap is a red test rather than a silent one. It was a second copy here.

import { Core, Cli } from "std/platform.wac";
